----- FILE: reference/sales-process.md -----
# How a Deal Moves

## The Three Steps of the Work

**Research → Validate → Build.** Nothing gets built on a guess. The buyer sees the work at each
step before the next one starts. Nobody is asked to trust a slide.

## The Discovery Call

Discovery and the pitch happen in the same call. Run it in this order:

1. **Current state.** How does the work run today? Who calls, what gets written on paper, where
   does a job fall through the cracks?
2. **Gap.** What does that cost them, in their own numbers?
3. **Solution.** The smallest working outcome that closes the gap.
4. **Next step.** Every call ends with one, written down: a date, an owner, and what happens.

Money comes up by the second conversation at the latest. Do not give away free strategy: the
call finds the problem; the Signal Audit or the proposal solves it.

## CRM Stages

Every deal sits in exactly one stage. Move it the day it changes.

| Stage | Means |
|---|---|
| Leads | Qualified with the three questions in `buyers.md`, no proposal yet |
| Proposals | A proposal has been sent |
| Closed Won | Signed agreement returned |
| Closed Lost | Said no, went quiet after the agreed follow-ups, or chose someone else. Log why. |

## Proposals

- Every proposal offers three options: Good, Better, Best.
- Each step up adds at least $1,000 and real value. Best is the target.
- Written in plain client language.
- The Founder & CEO sets the prices. The Head of Sales approves every proposal before it is sent.

## After a Yes

1. The agreement goes alone.
2. Only after the signed agreement comes back does the deposit invoice go out.
3. Payment: 50% at signing. Engagements of 90 days or more bill the balance monthly; shorter
   ones bill the balance at go-live.

Legal & Contracts sends the agreement. Finance & Billing sends the deposit invoice. The Sales
Executive tells the Head of Sales the buyer said yes, the same day.

----- FILE: reference/syllabus.md -----
# Syllabus

Training runs in two parts. **Initial Training** (days 1 to 10) ends at the **Launch Gate**. After
launch the new hire is in production, working live deals. **Continuing Education** (days 11 to
90) adds the advanced topics, one at a time.

Every module ends in a milestone. The Head of Sales signs off each milestone at a check-in. A
module is done when it is signed off, not when it is read.

Each module is taught the same way: **tell** (read the reference), **show** (watch the Head of
Sales do it, or read the example), **do** (try it), **check** (say it back, or do it for the Head
of Sales).

## Part 1: Initial Training (Days 1 to 10)

| # | Module | When | Reference | Milestone (signed off by the Head of Sales) |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | The Message | Day 1 | `the-message.md` | Say the one line and the arc from memory. Name three things never to say. |
| 2 | The Buyers | Day 1 | `buyers.md` | Name both buyers, who decides, who champions, and the three qualifying questions. Sort five sample businesses correctly. |
| 3 | The Work | Day 1 | `sales-process.md`, `case-study.md` | Explain Research, Validate, Build in your own words, using the case study. Name the four CRM stages. |
| 4 | Tools and Ownership | Day 2 | `tools.md`, `who-owns-what.md` | All access works. Revenue Operations walks you through the CRM screen; then log a practice call in it. Name the owning title for ten sample questions. |
| 5 | The Discovery Call | Days 3 to 7 | `sales-process.md`, `discovery-questions.md`, `templates.md` | Sit in on every discovery call the Head of Sales runs. Write research briefs on three assigned leads. Pass one practice call with the Head of Sales playing the owner: current state, gap, solution, next step. |
| 6 | Offers, Proof, and Objections | Days 8 to 10 | `offers.md`, `case-study.md`, `objections.md` | Name every public price and where it is shown. Tell the case study in two minutes. Handle five objections in a role-play. |

## The Launch Gate (End of Day 10)

The new hire is launched into production when all of these are true:

1. Modules 1 to 6 are signed off.
2. One live discovery call has been co-run with the Head of Sales, with the next step logged in
   the CRM the same day.
3. The Head of Sales says "launched" at the check-in.

**In production means:** you run live discovery calls with the Head of Sales on the line, keep
your deals in the CRM, quote public prices when asked, and draft follow-ups that the Head of Sales
approves before they go. If the gate is not met by day 10, the missing module is repeated. Nobody
is launched early.

## Part 2: Continuing Education (Days 11 to 90)

One advanced topic about every two weeks, while working live deals.

| # | Topic | When | Reference | Milestone (signed off by the Head of Sales) |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| CE1 | The Signal Audit in Depth | Weeks 3 to 4 | `offers.md` | Explain to a practice buyer what the audit returns and why it comes before any build. |
| CE2 | The Get-Me-Online Buyer | Weeks 3 to 4 | `buyers.md`, `offers.md` | Handle a practice Dig Drive DIY inquiry start to finish, using only the public prices. |
| CE3 | The Products | Weeks 5 to 6 | `offers.md` | Explain WalterFetch, BananaForce, and BananaBots in one sentence each, and say which buyer each fits. |
| CE4 | Referral Partners | Weeks 7 to 8 | `offers.md`, `who-owns-what.md` | Explain the partner offer and route a partner question to Partnerships. |
| CE5 | Three-Option Proposals | Weeks 9 to 10 | `sales-process.md` | Draft a Good, Better, Best proposal for a real deal, for the Founder & CEO to price. |
| CE6 | Your Own Pipeline | Weeks 11 to 12 | `buyers.md`, `discovery-questions.md` | Source and qualify your own leads with the three questions. Run discovery calls alone. |

## The 90-Day Review

The Head of Sales and the new hire review every milestone, the scorecard in `expectations.md`,
the CRM, and the deals worked.

----- FILE: reference/templates.md -----
# Templates and Examples

Copy these. Each template is followed by a filled example. The example business is made up for
practice; it is not a client.

## Research Brief (One Page, Before Every Call)

```
Business:            name, town, what they do, how many people
Which buyer:         1 (advisory) or 2 (get-me-online), and why
Who decides:         the owner-operator's name, if public
Likely champion:     the office manager or equivalent, if public
What they use now:   tools visible on their website or job posts
Why-now signals:     new location, hiring, reviews, a season coming
Three questions:     the first three you will ask, from discovery-questions.md
Sources:             every link you used
```

### Example: Brief

```
Business:            Example Heating & Air (practice only), Fort Wayne, residential HVAC, ~12 people
Which buyer:         1. Owner posts about growth and hiring; this is a workflow question, not a website.
Who decides:         The owner, named on the About page
Likely champion:     Office manager, named in a job post for a second dispatcher
What they use now:   Online booking widget on the site; job post asks for "spreadsheet skills"
Why-now signals:     Hiring a second dispatcher; heating season starting
Three questions:     #4 (call to job), #6 (falls through the cracks), #12 (why this quarter)
Sources:             company website, job post, Google Business Profile
```

## Call Note (Same Day, in the CRM)

```
Date and attendees:
Current state:       how the work runs today, in their words
Gap:                 what it costs them, with their numbers
Why now:
Decider / champion:
Next step:           DATE, OWNER, WHAT HAPPENS
Stage:               Leads / Proposals / Closed Won / Closed Lost
```

### Example: Call Note

```
Date and attendees:  Tue, owner and office manager (practice only)
Current state:       Calls go to a cell phone; jobs written on a whiteboard; invoices typed weekly
Gap:                 Owner estimates 3 missed callbacks a week in season; office manager re-keys every job
Why now:             Second dispatcher starts next month; wants one system before then
Decider / champion:  Owner decides; office manager is the champion
Next step:           Fri, Head of Sales, send Signal Audit overview (draft approved first)
Stage:               Leads
```

## Follow-Up Email (Draft for Approval)

```
Subject: Next step from our call

[Name], thank you for walking me through how [the work] runs today.

What I heard: [the gap, in their words].
What happens next: [the next step], by [date].
What I need from you: [one thing].

[Your name]
WalterSignal
```

Every draft goes to the Head of Sales before it is sent.

----- FILE: reference/the-message.md -----
# The Message

## The One Line

> The hardest part of AI isn't the technology. It's knowing what to ignore.

If the new hire remembers one thing from day one, it is this line. Simplicity is the message:
most owners are over-tooled, under-integrated, and tired of being sold to. WalterSignal wins by
being the simplest, clearest voice in the room.

## The Arc of Every Conversation

**Noise → Clarity → Accountability.**

1. **Noise.** The owner is buried in AI claims, tools, and vendor demos.
2. **Clarity.** WalterSignal says what is worth doing and what to skip, in plain words.
3. **Accountability.** One named person is answerable for the work. That is the product.

## What the Buyer Hears (Trust Order)

Accountability first, then trust, then judgment. Framework names and internal terms stay inside
the company. A buyer should never hear "ICM," "pipeline stage," or tool names they did not ask
about.

## Say This

- "We start with how your business actually runs."
- "You see it working on your own numbers before the rest is built."
- "You own it: the domain, the code, and the data are in your name."
- "No monthly fee for the software. No per-user fee."

## Never Say This

- Never claim WalterSignal publishes research. It does not. Sample reports are paid client work.
- Never lead a cold conversation with a voice agent, an "AI audit," an "assessment," or a
  "strategy session." Lead with a working outcome the buyer can picture.
- Never promise a speed or productivity multiple above 5×.
- Never discount. Discounts belong to the Founder & CEO, and only in exchange for something real.
- Never promise a delivery date. Dates come from Solutions Engineering after scoping.

